== Concepten, metingen en nummers <section:concepten-migratie>

=== Migrant =/ migreren <section:migrant-migreren>

The UN Migration Agency (International Organization for Migration) defines a migrant as any person who is moving or has moved across an international border or within a State away from his/her habitual place of residence, regardless of

(1) the person's legal status;

(2) whether the movement is voluntary or involuntary;

(3) what the causes for the movement are; or

(4) what the length of the stay is.

As such, migration is a sub-category of the more general concept of geographic mobility: mobility includes both circulation and migration, where the latter involves a change of habitual place of residence while the first does not (and also includes moving back and forth between different places for reasons of seasonal labor, for example).

This module addresses international migration rather than migration within the boundaries of nation states.


How many migrants are the in the world?

A simple question but there is no easy answer. A essential distinction to start counting is to distinguish between stock and flow statistics, as with any demographic measure.

    Stock statistics count the number of people with some characteristic at a given point in time in some geographic area.
    Flow statistics refer to the number of some kind of event observes during a period of time.

The number of migrants in a country on January 1, 2000, is an example of stock statistic. The number of immigrants who entered the country in the course of the year 2000 is an example of flow statistic. The distinction is crucial, since it is possible for a country to have a very low number of immigrants entering in a given year, but still have a very high number of migrants living in that country in that same year (or the other way around). 

In international statistics, an often-used definition to quantify the numer of migrants, is to count all people who live in a given country but who were not born there. Although alternative definitions might be considered better for certain purposes, this one works best for collecting comparable data across the globe. 

=== Migrante corridors <section:migrante-corridors>

In the past three decades, close to 3% of the world population was a migrant, according to the definition just given. This proportion has been growing slightly, which indicates that the population of migrants has grown a bit faster than the total world population.

This may be due to the ongoing migration transition (discussed in the video 'Migration hump theory') but migration experts also point out that the registration of migration has improved, so that some part of this growth is an artefact of the improved registration.

Anyway: there are no indications at all that there has been a dramatic rise of migration in recent years.

The available international migrant data include estimates of origin and destination links between two countries, allowing for the estimation of bilateral migration corridors globally. The size of a migration corridor from country A to country B is measured as the number of people born in country A who were residing in country B at the time of the estimate. Migrant corridors represent an accumulation of migratory movements over time and provide a snapshot of migration patterns have evolved into significant foreign-born populations in specific destination countries (International migrants: numbers and trends - World Migration Report (iom.int)).

For historical and recent data, check the yearly World Migration Reports of the International Organization for Migration and their data platform:

=== Refugees and asylum seekers <section:refugees-asylum-seekers>

When it comes to media reports on migration, a lot of attention goes to the challenges related to refugees. In quantitative terms, however, international refugees make up only about 10% of the total stock of international migrants worldwide - and this stock of migrants make up around 3% of the total world population, meaning that about 0.334% of the total world population is an international refugee.

    Refugee camp with children
    Who is a refugee is defined in international law. The 1951 Refugee Convention defines a refugee as a person who "owing the well-founded fear of being persecuted for reasons of race, religion, nationality, membership of a particular social group or political opinion, is outside the country of (their) nationality and is unable or, owing to such fear, is unwilling to avail (themself) of the protection of that country" (The 1951 Refugee Convention - UNHCR).
    An asylum-seeker is someone who intends to seek or is awaiting a decision on their request for international protection. In some countries, it is used as a legal term for a person who has applied for refugee status and has not yet received a final decision on their claim (Asylum-seekers - UNHCR).
    Internally displaced people or persons (IDPs) have been forced to flee their homes by conflict, violence, persecution or disasters, however, unlike refugees, they remain within their own country (Internally Displaced people - UNHCR)

By far the largest proportion of refugees and asylum seekers are hosted in what UNHCR calls developing countries, typically in a neighboring country of the country of origin. Data on asylum seekers, refugees as well as IDP's are collected bu the UN Refugee Agency, called the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees (UNHCR).

For recent data on these issues, go here: UNHCR Data - UNHCR.

