== Migratie policies <section:migratie-policies>
=== Introduction <section:introduction-migratie-policies>
In the initial stages of the development of modern nation states, (implicit) national migration policies were focused on trying to prevent citizens to migrate out to another country, as this would undermine the size and strength of the population, considered an important asset underpinning the wealth of nations.

However, the period after the First World War (1914-1918) gave rise to more restrictive migration policies, where governments were increasingly trying to monitor who was allowed to move in. This is the period when passport requirements were introduced to this end.

After the Second World War (1939-1945), many European nation states started to develop active labor immigration policies, since an enlarged labor force was deemed needed to rebuild the national infrastructures and economies. This post-war era was also the period when very high number of refugees were on the move, and the 1951 Refugee Convention, often called “the Geneva Convention” was signed (The 1951 Refugee Convention | UNHCR).

=== Landen en grenscontroles <section:landen-en-grenscontroles>

Since the latter quarter of the twentieth century, nation states and international bodies like the European Union have increasingly been focusing on external border control and limiting the number of immigrants.

In Europe, the 1985 Schengen Agreement represents an important turning point, as internal border control within the Schengen Area was abandoned, with increasing political concerns about external border control. Some political rhetoric even called for “closing the borders”. However, migration policies as border control politics have to face a number of substitution effects.

