Migration has become a hot and often politically polarizing topic, but it is absolutely not a new phenomenon at all. Actually, from its very first beginning, the history of humankind has been one of people on the move. Nevertheless, in today's globalized society, migration has taken on different and lager proportions.

The globalization of flows of information and capital influences migration flows, and migration flows influence the further course of globalization: modern transportation and communication technology facilitate migration over longer distances, and with current means of communication, it is much easier for migrants to stay in contact with their relatives in their home country, for instance.

Does this mean that "the world is becoming a village?". To some extent, yes, and for some people. But it does pose a number a major global challenges.

In this module we looked at the dimensions of major migration flows in the recent as well as more distant history. Next, we analysed theories that try to explain thema, discussed policy responses to migration and some aspects of the impact on societies.

Rather than focusing on specific issues in particular historical and political contexts, this lecture aims to address structural, long-term and global issues.
It has some clear connections with other modules covered in the KU Leuven Global challenges MOOC:

    One obvious connection is that human mobility is using modern transportation technology leads to emissions of greenhoud gasses, with an impact on climate change. This poses issues connected with the question of planetary boundaries.
    In turn, climate change could increasingly become a driver for migration. This is a topic explicitly adressed in one of the video's.
    If "the world is becoming a village", this holds only for a very small portion of the world population. The large majority does not have the financial means nor the political power to make use of modern transportation technology to move wherever they may want to go. There are clear connections with the topics of global governance and also with the module on economic inequality.
